Receipt: .codex/artifacts/claude-peer-loop-eneo-flows-production-roadmap-20260830T083348Z.md

Verified and incorporated:
- added explicit blocking dependencies and confirmed the graph is acyclic;
- split automatic Flow cleanup removal from the administrator purge transport;
- made abandoned runtime uploads an explicit purge category;
- kept a durable purge-operation ledger with one active operation per tenant/scope;
- bounded execution to the preview as-of and deterministic policy/scope fingerprint;
- made crash recovery failed_resumable and admin-triggered, never automatic;
- required operator identity for audit dead-letter redrive;
- separated failure injection, runbook/alert validation, and final release certification;
- narrowed performance work to changed or launch-critical workloads;
- kept UI authoring P0 and moved reload/reconnect to a dependent P1;
- documented that claimed webhook rows remain PENDING and must block stale recovery;
- required stale automatic-retention tests and product copy to be replaced.

Source verification:
- the reviewed Flow eligibility code does not implement classification, legal holds, or minimum-retention controls;
- the existing retention-governance E2E models automatic behavior absent from the reviewed page;
- no production operator caller exists for audit dead-letter redrive;
- the maintenance worker already supports explicit long-running functions;
- pending webhook claim behavior is asserted by integration tests.

Deliberate non-creation:
- no rerun-repository extraction task until a concrete duplication/change trigger exists;
- no shared Flow AI Builder cleanup task without an independently reviewed shared-contract baseline.
These choices prevent speculative architecture and avoid colliding with the Builder workstream.

description:
TL;DR: Own the single staging failure-injection campaign for Flow and produce reusable receipts for health/runbook and release certification.

## Problem

Unit/integration suites cover invariants, but production needs one traceable failure campaign at the final topology. Health proof and release certification must consume, not duplicate, this campaign.

## Canonical owner and approach

Test existing deep owners at public behavior boundaries: dispatch, executor checkpoints, terminalizer, review/rerun, webhook/audit outboxes, file binding/purge, publication fencing, and persisted parsers. No chaos framework.

## Acceptance Criteria

The campaign covers:
- broker timeout and ambiguous acceptance;
- duplicate dispatch;
- worker crash after each durable checkpoint;
- provider completion after timeout;
- webhook timeout/retry/claim expiry/dead letter;
- audit sink failure and operator redrive;
- storage failure around file binding and administrator-requested purge;
- corrupt supported/unsupported control envelope;
- review resume conflict;
- concurrent publish/edit;
- purge worker crash followed by explicit administrator resume.

Every case asserts persisted state, retry/idempotency, terminal outcome, audit evidence, and no active orphan attempt/result. Receipts name candidate SHA, image digests, topology, config, and time and are reused by flows-1z1.7 and flows-1z1.13.

## Tests and validation

Run targeted PostgreSQL/runtime suites, then identical cases in isolated staging with synchronization barriers/fault hooks, not sleeps. Retain machine-readable receipts and a concise verdict.

## Risk and recovery

Use disposable tenants and reversible injection. Restore each dependency and prove backlog convergence before continuing.

## Out of scope

No duplicate health campaign, event sourcing, generic state-machine, chaos platform, or production experiment.

## Evidence

Supplied review lines 874-887 and 1027-1035 enumerates required cases.
Confidence: high.
